        "Aysel had lived all her life in a warm coastal city where it almost never snowed. So when her father got a job in a "
        "mountain town in the north, she imagined winter as something from a film: soft white hills, hot chocolate and quiet "
        "evenings by the fire.\n\n"
        "The reality was different. In November the temperature dropped below zero, and Aysel discovered that her thin jacket "
        "was useless. Her fingers hurt when she walked to school, and the first time she stepped on ice, she fell and dropped "
        "all her books into a puddle. Her new classmates laughed, and she went home in tears, promising herself she would never "
        "go outside again until spring.\n\n"
        "The next morning there was a knock at the door. It was Elvin, a boy from her class, holding an old pair of boots. "
        "'These were my sister's,' he said. 'They have special soles. You won't fall in these.' He also showed her how to walk "
        "on ice — with small steps and bent knees, 'like a penguin'.\n\n"
        "By January Aysel had learned to ski a little, and she had built her first snowman with Elvin and his sister. When her "
